<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Suratte</h1><p class="st-meta">1616 CE – 1825 CE · capital Surat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch East India Company's trading establishment at Surat, the Mughal Empire's greatest port.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>) opened a factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> around 1616, shortly after the English, gaining access to the trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Surat became the headquarters of the directorate of Suratte, which also oversaw posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Cambay</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth and saltpetre for export to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, competing closely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>. The factory was a commercial establishment, not a territorial state, operating under Mughal permission and local governors. It declined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> sacks of 1664 and 1670.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat's</a> trade shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in the 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> presence dwindled. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> held the post during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> and, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a>, it was formally ceded in 1825.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dutch-suratte.jpg" alt="Dutch Suratte" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_East_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1616 CE – 1825 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Surat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dutch Surat</dd></dl></aside></div>

